docs[1]{path,scope,note}:
  docs/MERGE_SAFETY.md,project,audited branch merge installation receipts reconciliation and CI contract
close_reason: "Delivered a fresh-clone-safe, extension-independent CI merge bootstrap plus exact committed-fence assertion; exact static command set passes locally after the PR-head failure."
body: "## Evidence\n\n`.github/workflows/ci.yml:211-217`, the job step named **\"Run tracker merge-safety gates\"**:\n\n```yaml\nnode dist/cli.js validate --check-storage-integrity --json\nrepresentative_id=\"$(node dist/cli.js list --status all --limit 1 --sort created_at --order desc --json | jq -er '.items[0].id')\"\nnode dist/cli.js history \"${representative_id}\" --verify --strict-exit --json > /dev/null\n```\n\nTwo measurable holes, both in the step whose name promises merge safety.\n\n**One stream of 2,058.** `pm stats` on this workspace reports 2,058 history streams. The gate verifies the hash chain of exactly one — the most recently created item, which by construction is the item *least* likely to have participated in a historical merge, because it has the shortest history and the fewest branches that could have touched it. Coverage is 0.05%, and it is adversely selected.\n\n**Drift is not checked at all.** The `validate` invocation passes `--check-storage-integrity` and omits `--check-history-drift`. Storage integrity answers \"is this file parseable and well-formed\"; drift answers \"does the item on disk match what its history says it should be\". They are different questions and only the second one detects the failure mode below.\n\n## The failure this lets through, reproduced end to end\n\nA two-branch divergence was constructed in a scratch repository on 2026-07-26 with the shipped 2026.7.26 build and `pm merge install` applied:\n\n1. `main` creates an item and commits.\n2. `agentA` adds a comment and sets `priority: 1`; commits.\n3. `agentB` adds a different comment and sets `estimated_minutes: 60`; commits.\n4. `main` merges A (fast-forward), then merges B.\n\nThe field-aware driver did its job and reported success — `fields_from_theirs: [estimated_minutes]`, `union_fields: [comments]`, history `strategy: union_reanchor, entries_total: 5`. `git merge` exited 0 with no conflict. The merged item correctly carries `priority: 1` *and* `estimated_minutes: 60` *and* both comments.\n\nThen:\n\n```\npm history <id> --verify  ->  ok: false, errors: [\"verify_failed:current_item_hash_mismatch\"]\npm validate --check-history-drift  ->  drifted_items_count: 1, hash_mismatches: 1\n```\n\nA clean, conflict-free, correct merge leaves the workspace failing its own integrity check. That is `pm-9j2r3b`'s subject and its acceptance criteria already require the fix. This item is the other half: **CI cannot see it happen.** The drift check is absent, and the one stream that is verified is the newest item, not the merged one.\n\nScale the reproduction to this repository's own workflow — many agents, many branches, thousands of streams — and a merge wave could drift hundreds of streams while the merge-safety gate reports green on its single sample.\n\n## Why sampling is the wrong shape here, specifically\n\nSampling is defensible when the population is homogeneous and the cost of full coverage is prohibitive. Neither holds:\n\n- The population is *not* homogeneous. Drift is concentrated in exactly the items that merges touched, and the sampling rule (`--sort created_at --order desc --limit 1`) selects against them.\n- Full coverage is already implemented and already fast. `pm validate --check-history-drift` walked all 2,048 items in this workspace inside the same command that CI is already invoking. There is no budget argument for checking one.\n\nThis repository has already ruled on the general principle in `pm-w13j` — a curated or sampled proxy is not an acceptable substitute for a completeness gate when the complete check is affordable. That ADR was about coverage instrumentation; the reasoning transfers without modification.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- The merge-safety gate verifies **every** history stream, not a representative. The natural implementation is `pm validate --check-history-drift` on the workspace, which already reports `drifted_items` with counts split by `missing_streams`, `unreadable_streams`, `hash_mismatches`, and `chain_mismatches`.\n- `--strict-exit` semantics apply to the workspace-wide result, so a drifted stream anywhere fails the pull-request path rather than only the nightly path.\n- The step keeps a per-stream deep verification for at least one item so the `pm history --verify --strict-exit` code path itself stays exercised, but that is a smoke test of the command and is named as such, not as the coverage.\n- A negative-control fixture — a workspace with one deliberately drifted stream — proves the gate fails, satisfying `pm-k6t4yb`'s requirement that every enforced gate ship with proof that it can fail.\n- Because a clean merge currently produces drift by design (`pm-9j2r3b`), landing this gate before that fix would red-line the branch. The sequencing is explicit: this gate lands behind `pm-9j2r3b`, or lands simultaneously with `pm merge reconcile` wired into the post-merge path so the invariant is true before it is enforced.\n\n## Relationship to neighbours\n\n- `pm-9j2r3b` fixes the cause (a merge writes no history entry, so the merged state is unaddressable and the stream hash cannot match). This item fixes the detection. Its acceptance criteria already state \"a clean merge leaves pm validate with zero history-drift warnings\" — that sentence is only meaningful if something runs `pm validate` in CI, which today nothing does.\n- `pm-kpftft` builds the ratcheted per-warning baseline for `pm validate` and `pm health` against this repository's own workspace. History drift is a hard failure rather than a ratcheted warning class, so this gate is a distinct binary check, but both should be produced by the same CI step and should share the same negative-control fixture harness.\n- `pm-c0wthb` covers the merge fence failing open on unresolvable driver output. That is a different failure at the same seam: fence fails open leaves unmarked conflicts, this gate fails blind leaves undetected drift.\n- `pm-76dnfg` builds randomized N-branch merge conformance with a zero-conflict acceptance bar. That is the property suite; this is the always-on gate against real merges. The property suite should reuse this gate's assertion so the two cannot disagree about what \"merged cleanly\" means.\n- `pm-iwsj` (closed) adopted the merge-safety gates into this repository. This item is recorded as `discovered_from` it: the gate shipped in a shape that reads complete and measures 0.05%."
